**Dual-write (phase 1).** Legacy `pg-orders-1` remains the system of record and serves every read and write. Each write also commits an `orders_outbox` row in the same transaction. `outbox-relay` then applies those rows to orders-v2 asynchronously, and `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders each night. Nothing reads orders-v2 yet, so errors there are invisible to customers; the hourly `order-diff` checker exists to catch them. The phase passes when four things hold:
- the backfill is complete;
- the mismatch rate stays under 0.01% for 3 consecutive days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds for the same 3 days;
- a one-time full-table checksum matches, which is the only check that covers every old order.

**Cutover (phase 2).** Reads move first. `orders.read_from_v2` ramps from 1% to 10%, 50% and 100%, one step a day, and then holds at 100% for a 48-hour soak. Writes keep going to both stores during this time, so rollback only means setting the flag back to 0. After the soak, `orders.write_legacy` is turned off and orders-v2 becomes the only store receiving writes. That step is one-way because nothing syncs back to legacy, so it needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director. Legacy then stays read-only for 30 days before it is decommissioned under `CHG-4471`. The cutover counts as done after 7 days with no `ORD-4xx` incident and order-history p99 at or under 180 ms.

The schedule has about one day of slack. The soak ends on 2026-11-29 and the write stop is set for 2026-11-30. If a ramp step holds for more than a day, the write stop falls into the code freeze that starts on 2026-12-01, and dual-write continues until January.

**Dual-write (phase 1).** Legacy `pg-orders-1` stays the source of truth for every read and write. Each write also commits an `orders_outbox` row in the same legacy transaction, and `outbox-relay` (6 replicas, idempotent on `(order_id, version)`) applies those rows to orders-v2 in commit order. Meanwhile `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders each night from 01:00 to 05:00 UTC, which takes about 11 nights. Nothing reads orders-v2 yet, so customers can't see any errors in it. The phase passes only when four things hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- `order-diff` mismatches stay under 0.01% for 3 consecutive days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds for those same 3 days;
- a one-time full-table checksum matches. This is the only check that covers all old orders, because the hourly checker only samples them.

**Cutover (phase 2).** Reads move first. `orders.read_from_v2` steps through 1%, 10%, 50% and 100%, one step a day, and both stores keep receiving writes. Reads then stay at 100% on orders-v2 for a 48-hour soak. Up to this point, rollback means setting the flag back to 0, which takes effect within a minute and loses nothing. Next comes the write stop: `orders.write_legacy` is turned off and `outbox-relay` is scaled to zero. This step is one-way, because nothing syncs orders-v2 back to legacy, so it needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director. Legacy then stays up read-only for 30 days and is decommissioned under `CHG-4471`. The cutover counts as done after 7 days with no `ORD-4xx` incident and an order-history p99 at or under 180 ms.

The timeline has no slack. The ramp, the soak and the write stop fill November 24–30 exactly, so if any ramp step is held for even one day, the write stop slips into the code freeze. It would then wait until January, with dual-write running the whole time.

**Soft-delete window (30 days).** Deleting an account sets `accounts.deleted_at` and removes nothing. The account disappears from the product because all queries read through `active_accounts`, which filters out rows where `deleted_at` is set. At the same time, login is refused, sessions are revoked, email is suppressed, and pending export links expire. For 30 days, support can undo the deletion with `acctctl restore <account_id>` once the user passes the `ID-RB-12` identity check. The user then logs in with their old password, but their old sessions are not restored. The window exists because about 4% of deletions are reversed, and because support needs a way to undo deletions made by someone who took over an account.

**Hard purge.** `purge_accounts` runs nightly at 02:30 UTC. Each night it:

- picks up to 5,000 accounts whose `deleted_at` is more than 30 days old;
- deletes their rows from 14 tables, children first, in the order set by `purge_order.yaml`;
- tags their objects in `user-media-prod` so a lifecycle rule deletes them within 24 hours;
- writes a tombstone to `purge_ledger`, which holds no personal data and is kept for 7 years.

A failed account is retried on the next three nights; a fourth failure raises `PRV-104`. At 04:00 UTC, `purge_verify` confirms that the tables are empty and the tombstones exist, and raises `PRV-105` if not. After the purge, the account cannot be restored.

**Backups.** Database snapshots are kept for 35 days and never rewritten. A purged account therefore survives in backups for up to 65 days after the deletion request, and legal gives the user that backup expiry date. Restoring a backup requires replaying `purge_ledger` first (runbook `DB-RB-03`), so that purged accounts don't come back.

One caveat: the 65-day figure only holds if the account is purged on the first night it qualifies. The 5,000-a-night cap and the three retry nights can delay a purge. The rollout backlog of about 61,000 accounts will take at least 13 nights to clear, so early purges will run late. The date given to the user should therefore be computed from the actual purge time in the ledger, not from the deletion date plus 65 days.
